<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Walid I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/705">705</a>–<a href="/years/715">715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Walid I was an Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> who reigned from 705 to 715. His reign saw the greatest expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, including the conquests of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a>. He also sponsored major building projects, most notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Great Mosque of Damascus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-walid-i.jpg" alt="al-Walid I" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Not credited · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_dinar_of_al-Walid_707-708_CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Al-Walid was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in about 674, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</a>, a member of the Umayyad family's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> line. His mother, Wallada bint al-Abbas ibn al-Jaz, descended from a 6th-century chief of the Banu Abs. In 684 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> of the Hejaz were expelled by the rival claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-ibn-al-zubayr">Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr</a> and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where his grandfather Marwan I was recognised as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. As a prince, al-Walid led summer campaigns against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> in 696, 697, 698 and 699, and about 700 or 701 he patronised a fortified outpost, Qasr Burqu', on the desert route towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>. He became heir apparent about 705, after the death of Abd al-Malik's brother Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Al-Walid acceded on 9 October 705 and continued his father's policies of centralisation and expansion. Unlike Abd al-Malik, he depended heavily on the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a>, who ran the eastern half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> and influenced appointments and dismissals. In the east al-Hajjaj's lieutenant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutayba-ibn-muslim">Qutayba ibn Muslim</a> campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> between 705 and 715, winning the surrender of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Farghana</a>, mostly through tributary alliances that left local rulers in place; after Qutayba's death in 716 local princes and the Turgesh nomads rolled back most of the gains. From 708 or 709 Muhammad ibn al-Qasim conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a>. In the west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Hispania</a>, and the caliphate reached its largest territorial extent.</p><p>War spoils paid for building projects, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-mosque">Great Mosque of Damascus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anjar">Anjar</a>. He is described as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> to set up programmes of social welfare, aiding poor and disabled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. His reign was marked by domestic peace and prosperity, and he kept a balance between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qays">Qays</a> and Yaman army factions. The cost of the wars, and his grants to Umayyad princes, burdened his successors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Walid died of an illness at Dayr Murran, an Umayyad winter estate outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, on 23 February 715, about a year after al-Hajjaj. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-ibn-abd-al-aziz">Umar ibn Abd al-Aziz</a> led the funeral prayers. He had tried to nominate his son Abd al-Aziz in place of his brother Sulayman, whom his father had designated; Sulayman acceded and dismissed nearly all his governors, and expansion largely halted. Gerald Hawting calls the reigns of al-Walid and Abd al-Malik in some ways the high point of Umayyad power, and the historian Kennedy calls al-Walid's reign perhaps the zenith, though his own part in its successes is unclear. His Syrian contemporaries held him in high esteem.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Walid I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Mosque of Damascus was built in the reign of his son <b>al-Walid I</b> (r. 705–715).</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in Ghouta, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=710" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5603 1263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of al-Walid I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6053 1533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6053 1533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=710">What was here in 710 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
